<UserControl xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             xmlns:d="http://schemas.microsoft.com/expression/blend/2008"
             xmlns:mc="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/markup-compatibility/2006"
             mc:Ignorable="d" d:DesignWidth="392"
             x:Class="Remex.Desktop.Views.DialogContent">

    <!-- Title + wrapped message for the simple confirm/restore prompts. No view model - both
         MaterialDialogs.ConfirmAsync and RestoreAsync only ever have a title and message string, so
         the two TextBlocks below are named and set directly in code-behind, the same pattern the
         Window subclasses this replaced used (ConfirmationDialog.axaml.cs, RestorePromptWindow.axaml.cs).
         MaxWidth on the message is the fix round 1 fix: Material.Avalonia's own AlertDialog renders its
         SupportingText in a TextBlock with no TextWrapping set at all, which clips a message longer
         than the dialog's width instead of wrapping it - that control is not used here at all, so its
         inability to wrap cannot leak into this dialog.

         RemEx-x6a70.3 fix round 2: the button row below is real RemEx Buttons, not Material.Avalonia's
         DialogButtons - MaterialDialogs.cs hands CustomDialogBuilderParams an empty DialogButtons array
         so the library renders no button of its own (see that file's type remarks for why a
         library-rendered button cannot carry this app's Classes vocabulary against 3.19.0). Cancel is
         always "secondary"; ActionButton's Classes="primary" below is only the DEFAULT - the
         constructor clears it and applies the caller's own classes (ConfirmAsync passes "primary
         danger", RestoreAsync passes "primary"), so ButtonVocabularyTests sees a real Classes
         attribute (RemEx-z7pnx.1) without risking two emphases stacking if a future caller ever
         forgot to pass one. -->
    <!-- RemEx-df08 fix round 1: an AutomationProperties-attached Name value USED to live here, on
         this StackPanel. It announced nothing - Control.OnCreateAutomationPeer defaults to
         NoneAutomationPeer for any element with no dedicated peer, and Avalonia has no
         PanelAutomationPeer, so the name was set on a peer documented as "not contributing to the
         logical structure of the application" and a screen reader never saw it. The real fix is on
         the host WINDOW, not this content: MaterialDialogs.ConfirmAsync/RestoreAsync now call
         AutomationProperties.SetName(window, title) after building this control, because
         WindowAutomationPeer is a real peer that is actually announced. -->
    <StackPanel Spacing="18" Margin="24,24,24,24">
        <StackPanel Spacing="6">
            <TextBlock x:Name="HeaderText"
                       Theme="{StaticResource Headline6TextBlock}"/>
            <TextBlock x:Name="MessageTextBlock"
                       Theme="{StaticResource Body2TextBlock}"
                       Foreground="{DynamicResource TextSecondaryBrush}"
                       TextWrapping="Wrap"
                       MaxWidth="440"/>
        </StackPanel>
        <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" HorizontalAlignment="Right" Spacing="8">
            <Button x:Name="CancelButton" Classes="secondary" Click="OnCancelClick"/>
            <!-- Enter confirms (RemEx-df08), but only when the caller says the action is safe to
                 fire on a reflex - IsDefault is set in code-behind (DialogContent.axaml.cs), NOT
                 hardcoded here, because it has to be a per-call-site decision: ConfirmAsync's action
                 is "primary danger" (a destructive confirm, e.g. "Forget device?"), and a shared
                 IsDefault="True" here would let Enter fire that destructively for every caller,
                 including one who was just holding the key down. FIX ROUND 1 (RemEx-df08) found this
                 exact bug - see DialogContent.axaml.cs's constructor remarks for which callers get
                 the default and why. IsDefault still walks up to whatever Window this button ends up
                 inside at runtime and raises Click - confirmed against Avalonia's own docs - so it
                 reaches OnActionClick regardless of which builder call site created the host window,
                 same as before; only whether it is *set* moved. CancelButton stays plain: Escape
                 already closes the host window and resolves to a decline via
                 MaterialDialogs.AttachEscapeDismiss, and duplicating that with IsCancel here is
                 unnecessary. -->
            <Button x:Name="ActionButton" Classes="primary" Click="OnActionClick"/>
        </StackPanel>
    </StackPanel>
</UserControl>
